/** Editorial translations for siman 198 failing blocks — tevilah and chatzitzah */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '3#_': `From tightening. That she should not choke herself — and "katla" is what they call these three words in Ashkenazi pronunciation.`,
    '11#_': `In the eye. And there is no distinction here between within three days or after three days — so wrote Beit Yosef and Darkei Moshe, and not like Bach — end quote Siftei Kohen.`,
    '22#_': `She needs. Maharam of Lublin wrote in responsum — since this is not from law but from stringency, it appears they were stringent only when she was found to have forgotten one nail immediately after immersion before she slept with her husband; but if she did not find it until the next day, one should not be stringent lest he cast aspersion on her relations, and even if she did not have relations with her husband that night the matter is unseemly, and she needs no other immersion if no filth was found under it — end quote; and Siftei Kohen wrote on this — and it appears where she can return and immerse, even if she did not find until the next day and even if she says with certainty there was no filth under it, she should return and immerse because the nail itself interposes even without any mud, and it seems to me so is practiced to rule; but where not possible one should not be stringent since the night passed; and Taz wrote that if she already had relations she should not immerse again lest she conceived, and if you come to be stringent requiring second immersion you cast aspersion on that child (and all this when there is no excrement or dough under it; but if there is under it, even if she had relations with her husband she needs another immersion per law) — and he wrote further that if her immersion occurred on chol hamoed, Mordechai wrote she should not cut them herself but through a gentile maidservant; but we hold in Orach Chayyim siman 532 that she may cut them without any change (and Mordechai was stringent only through herself not through gentile maidservant); but on Shabbat and Yom Tov it appears (if it happened she forgot to take nails on Shabbat and Yom Tov she should not tell a gentile to cut for her, since there is no absolute mitzvah in taking nails for one can scrape under them — why nullify rabbinic Shabbat prohibition of telling a gentile; and further a woman assists by lowering her hand to him and it is as if she does melachah on Shabbat and Yom Tov — end quote) she should not cut at all even through gentile but scrape them well; and I saw one who erred and commanded cutting on Yom Tov through gentile and did not intend correctly — end quote (and Nekudot HaKesef challenged him at length and concluded one may tell gentile to take them for they did not decree rabbinic prohibition in place of mitzvah; and so ruled Magen Avraham siman 343 one may tell gentile to cut them for assisting has no substance; and in responsum part 98 question 2 he challenged Siftei Kohen in Nekudot HaKesef and agreed with Taz since it is stringent) — nevertheless since one can take them without a utensil why be lenient with a utensil; but without utensil the matter is plain permitted and no law requires six — end quote; see there.`,
    '31#_': `In water. Siftei Kohen wrote — specifically in mikveh water but not in drawn water; but Taz wrote she may rinse her hands even in drawn water (for now that they are connected to the mikveh they are also valid for drawn water that is wet enough to wet another is connection) — and not as Rama wrote in siman 120 seif 2; and he wrote further regarding what Mechaber wrote that loosening helps and afterward wrote that rinsing hands helps even without loosening — nevertheless she must not hold her with force for otherwise rinsing first does not help, only we are not concerned for this — end quote; and Darkei Moshe wrote there is complete permission that when the woman immerses and her friend pushes her in water she may hold her by the hands at time of pushing if she rinsed her hands in water first and need not separate her hands from her — and not as our women practice separating their hands from her at time of pushing; and Siftei Kohen wrote that in his humble opinion one should not rule leniently in matters they practiced as prohibition, for in mishnah and poskim it is only permitted after the fact but l'chatchila who will tell us; however for a woman who cannot stand on her feet he ruled in responsum Maharashbal siman 81 that she should be immersed by two women holding her by the arms and they rinse their hands first in water until wet enough to wet another, but she has no remedy to lie her on a sheet and immerse her for a sheet receives tumah and is forbidden (as below seif 31) — end quote.`,
    '33#_': `That the water. Taz wrote — for Rambam and Ramban who hold above that even if holding with force it does not interpose if he rinsed his hands first — if so there is no distinction here whether they are moist from mikveh water or from other drawn water — see there; and Siftei Kohen wrote — doubtful, for here it implies when there is mud there she must raise her feet and may immerse there; and in siman 33 he ruled when there is mud there she should not immerse at all; and per what I wrote below that there is distinction between mud and mud it fits well — end quote.`,
    '35#_': `Planks. Taz wrote — and one should greatly wonder at the custom of our country making planks in the mikveh for the immersing woman to stand on; Levush greatly extended this wonder; and Maharam of Padua preceded him in responsum when Maharar David Vitel asked him why they do so and he answered at length — main point of our construction is that our planks in the mikveh are not considered designated for treading since they are not made to stand on them to immerse, for on the contrary thereby they leave the category of vessel and are considered like roof of house, especially after fixed with nails since initially there was no vessel on them — end quote; and further it appears one can find permission even when not fixed with nails, etc. see there he extends on this and concludes — and all I wrote regarding permission of planks applies also to wooden beams in mikveh found in all our country that one memory rises here and there — end quote; and Maharam of Lublin wrote in responsum — certainly one should not change the custom lest he cast aspersion on the early ones; nevertheless whenever it happens before me they repair or build a new mikveh I always command fixing it with things not fit for treading so it be valid per all — end quote.`,
    '39#_': `That. Siftei Kohen wrote — although above seif 14 he ruled only potter's clay interposes, etc. — one may say here too it deals with mikveh that has thick mud like potter's clay, and perhaps on this they now rely immersing in mikveh that has mud because ordinary mud is not like potter's clay.`,
    '40#_': `Counted for her. Siftei Kohen wrote — doubtful on this; and likewise regarding squeezing eyes excessively in siman 39 — some say immersion counted for her, and this is not per what Mechaber wrote above regarding kohl and blood in a wound that interposes, for these depend on each other per the text in Sha"s and as evident from all poskim — end quote; and Taz wrote — and it is hard for me why he invalidates after the fact; per those who say "because water precedes" we should also say so here; and one may say here there is concern wrinkles on body above water will stick, so when they enter water they are already stuck; per this if she inserted herself until her neck in water first it is immersion after the fact in every case; and it appears correct for every woman to do so for at least there is some advantage if she stays long without awareness — end quote (and Nekudot HaKesef resolves — there when she inserts feet in water her feet already touched water before reaching mud and that water is connected to mikveh therefore no interposition; but here through wrinkles water inside wrinkles will not be connected to mikveh at all — understand well — end quote).`,
    '43#_': `She needs. Siftei Kohen wrote — however all this does not prevent after the fact, as Bach too — end quote.`,
    '47#_': `These. Such as dog or donkey or am ha'aretz or gentile or pig or horse or metzora and the like — so Hagahos Sha"d; but in Rokeach and Kol Bo it states if a horse encounters her she conceives and has relations, her sons stand well in speech, hear and understand, learn Torah and do not forget, diminish sleep, and further their fear falls on creatures, etc. — end quote; and we learn in Pesachim — one who encounters a woman at time she ascends from obligatory immersion — if he preceded and had relations, a spirit of promiscuity for him; if she preceded and had relations, a spirit of promiscuity for her — what is her remedy? She should say thus: "He pours contempt upon nobles" (Tehillim 107) — and Rokeach brings it so; but in Toledot Aharon he did not cite verse "He pours contempt" but "and loosens the belt of the strong" (Iyov 12) — appears he did not have text in Sha"s "He pours contempt," etc.; and in Yalkut Sha"s this is brought in Tehillim and Iyov — therefore we teach both — end quote Siftei Kohen.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `In Torat Kohanim parshat Emor chapter 4 they derive thus — as written "but if he wash his flesh in water and the sun come upon him he shall be clean" — just as his sun comes all at once, so his coming in water all at once.`,
    '3#_': `Rosh there in his rulings in name of Raavad, and so Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba and Sha"s.`,
    '5#_': `Tosafot there (72b) [72b] and so Rosh.`,
    '6#_': `Statement of Rav Huna, and as Rav Yosef explains the reason there.`,
    '8#_': `Question of Rav Kahana from Rav there, and it was resolved.`,
    '23#_': `There in mishnah and as girsa, and scab on a wound, and per Rosh's explanation there.`,
    '24#_': `There mishnah 4 and per Rosh's explanation in chapter 10 of Niddah from that which Rami bar Chama — these scabs of ringworm up to three days, etc.`,
    '35#_': `Rambam there section 2 (and Rabbeinu Yerucham) statement of Rav Dimi as explained there Menachot 21a, and doubtful on Semag.`,
    '36#_': `Rosh there in chapter 10 of Niddah and so Rashba — because they are not particular, and on the contrary they return and renew them, and further there is no substance there only appearance.`,
    '41#_': `Rosh there and Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba and Sha"s.`,
    '42#_': `Mordechai chapter 2 of Shevuot from law of arrow, and I cited it above in seif 11.`,
    '47#_': `From incident with Rabbi's maidservant, etc. Niddah 66b — although we do not require water enter the mouth, nevertheless we require the mouth be fit to insert water, and per R. Zeira "all fit," etc. there and Menachot 18b.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#ב': `If, etc. even, etc. From what is stated in Tosefta brought by Rosh chapter 9 of Mikvaot — ornaments, etc. and rings, etc.; and Raavad challenged for its minority is not majority; and answer — since she is particular at time of kneading; and Rosh chapter 10 siman 26 and likewise wool and linen threads Rosh wrote there siman 25 that she is particular at time of washing, and so at end of Teshuvot HaRashba see there.`,
    '1#ג': `Or even, etc. In chapter 9 of Mikvaot mishnah 2 in Torat Kohanim for woman and mishnah 3 in Torat Kohanim for man — one, etc.; from which for Tanna Kamma there is no distinction except per way of majority of women, etc. — this is their dispute, and so in Teshuvot HaRashba.`,
    '2#א': `And if they are within, etc. Rosh end of chapter 6 of Shabbat siman 61 and so language of mishnah, etc.`,
    '5#א': `And it is, etc. until most, etc. As above siman 61 and Rashi in Eruvin 2b s.v. rovo, etc. and Semag; and although Rashi follows his view that there is no interposition in flesh, Tosafot disagree as written s.v. da'at, etc. and Semag — nevertheless in this most poskim agree to Rashi that majority of hair alone interposes.`,
    '6#א': `For married woman, etc. Like Rash in Tosefta brought by Rosh and Rosh there and Sha"s; and doubtful why he ruled like Rash when we hold halachah is not like him except in two matters as in chapter 4 of Chullin (82b); and possibly he explains Rash explains words of Sages — doubtful.`,
    '6#ב': `And those who have, etc. As above siman 65 that two hairs do not interpose; and further since impossible to remove them it is rabbinic extension as in chapter 8 of Yevamot (88) regarding fetus and likewise in vessels — Tosefta brought by Rosh chapter 10 of Mikvaot mishnah 1 — all handles of vessels inserted in their normal way, etc. not in normal way and reason of not normal way is that he will remove them. Mordechai end of Hilchos Niddah see there.`,
    '7#_': `Eye-mucus, etc. Tosafot siman 67 section 1 s.v. lefelof, etc.`,
    '14#_': `However if, etc. From what is stated end of chapter 5 of Chullin — heavy water and lower residents, etc.; and in Zevachim 88b — bucket full of spittle, etc. and Rashi there and Tosafot s.v. deli, etc. and see Siftei Kohen.`,
    '20#_': `Specifically dough, etc. From seif 18 mentioned there — excrement under, etc. — specifically the excrement.`,
    '21#_': `Its majority. In Tosefta brought by Rosh there and Sha"s and like R. Yonatan ben Yosef; and he holds he explains words of Rabbis; but Rambam ruled like mishnah and did not distinguish; and see Bach who extended on this.`,
    '24#_': `(Extract) And there are women who practice not, etc. As stated chapter 8 of Chullin (105a) — one who ate meat is forbidden, etc.; and Rambam explained because meat remains between teeth; and as Shulchan Aruch wrote above siman 89 see there (end).`,
    '25#א': `And if not, etc. From that of Rabbi's maidservant mentioned there — reason bone of gentile is not. There.`,
    '28#_': `Unless she loosened, etc. and if, etc. Like Tanna Kamma and Avi HaEzri; and wrote in Teshuvot HaRashba but Ramban wrote opposite that Tanna Kamma is stringent, etc.; and another girsa from what is stated chapter 4 of Yevamot (46a) — they loosened for him, etc.; and in Tosefta ornaments, etc. as above siman 23 except R. Shimon is stringent, etc.`,
    '31#א': `One does not immerse, etc. As stated Pesachim 17b — R. Ashi in impure vessels, etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. ella, etc.; and one may say they decreed, etc.; and further in many places; and as I wrote therefore, etc.`,
    '31#ג': `She should not stand, etc. From mishnah chapter 5 of Mikvaot mishnah 2 provided, etc.; and Rosh there — therefore must say from decree reason, etc.; and Raavad wrote — therefore it says in Niddah there regarding earthenware vessel, etc. because it does not become impure from back and also not impure from treading as in chapter 9 of Shabbat (84); and likewise silta is not impure at all; but wooden vessel even from back (or matter) fit for treading there is concern of decree of bathhouses like bench of that mishnah; and likewise planks and similar of that mishnah — impossible to establish from fear reason for it speaks of vessels; perforce must say from bathhouses reason; and practical difference also in person — for if from fear reason specifically l'chatchila but from bathhouses reason even after the fact does not help; and so Rashba and Ran; but view of Rosh and Rambam is bathhouses reason does not exist in conclusion and that mishnah specifically on back of vessels for they decreed because of inside; and see Rosh end siman 29 provided she did not, etc.; and some say, etc.`,
    '35#א': `She should not immerse, etc. So explained in Teshuvot HaRashba as stated there derech gedilatah: (Extract) she should not immerse, etc. — language of Teshuvot HaRashba and his view (above note 44) and view of Raavad that R. Eliezer came to be stringent and support for R. Yochanan (end).`,
    '35#ב': `At time, etc. Tosafot there s.v. umenika, etc. and so Rosh in mishnah there and Sha"s and chapter like Tanna Kamma.`,
    '35#ג': `And need not, etc. So Rosh explained derech gedilatah and Rashi there s.v. lo titbol, etc.: (Extract) and need not distance, etc. — language of Rosh and his view that R. Eliezer came to be lenient and reject those statements as above (in extract in note 44); and Rashi explained there s.v. lo titbol stringently and his view another girsa and halachah is not, etc.; but for practical law no difference in all this (end).`,
    '36#_': `Must be, etc. For otherwise she bends much and must be derech gedilatah as above siman 35; and see Yoma 78a and must say until neck specifically need not; and Rashba wrote this in chapter 7 of Mikvaot — mikveh whose waters are shallow, etc.`,
    '37#_': `There is water, etc. For in such case too she may immerse derech gedilatah; and in Tosefta brought by Rosh chapter 10 mishnah 4 — one who binds hands and feet, etc.; and so Rambam.`,
    '39#_': `Some say she should not, etc. Tosafot siman 67 section 1 s.v. petachah, etc.; and Semag and Mordechai wrote even per Tosafot view this is only after the fact but l'chatchila even for her husband; and per Rashi view even after the fact not as written "did not count," etc.; and on this depends dispute of siman 35 and Bach 72b who wrote several views on Tosafot girsa — understand; and see above seifim 8 and 9 and Siftei Kohen siman 49: (Extract) some say she should not, etc. — Tosafot there s.v. petachah, etc.; but Rabbeinu Tam explained, etc. and so in Rif and Raavad explained it refers only to scab of ringworm and below; and on three topics — scab, eye-mucus, and kohl — halachah is only matter of R. Yochanan itself, etc.; and he brings proof on R. Yochanan that it is halachah but first topics were stated only for purity; and he says interposes and does not interpose; and in topic of R. Yochanan he says she did not count, etc. — clearly dealing with woman; and listener heard and erred and established also for first topics in woman — but not except eye-mucus and even dry and kohl even on eye and scab even after three does not interpose [and thereby all Rashi's difficulties in that Tosafot are resolved]; and Raavad's reason who explained specifically on those three where feminine language was not mentioned unlike those where feminine language — especially in R. Yochanan's matter which comes to support R. Eliezer as Raavad wrote what R. Eliezer stated, etc. has no dawn to reject those statements for R. Eliezer came to be stringent — rather to support R. Yochanan; and so Rashba. But Rambam explained from when she puts food for nursing and onward; and even R. Yochanan's matter lacks word lefelof in Rambam's words (or lefelof or, etc. and so in manuscript); and so he arranged all those together — Niddah, etc. secretion, etc. and likewise eye-mucus, etc. kohl, etc. opened, etc. alternatively, etc.; and those above he did not arrange there; and Rambam's girsa that disqualifies before earthenware vessel after she immersed and counted, etc. — there this statement applies and therefore he explained from nursing food for from filing and above impossible for filing is certainly halachah from Shmuel's father as Rashi's difficulty brought in Tosafot there. And Tosafot view refers from earthenware vessel onward and also R. Yochanan as above; and Tosafot 66b s.v. im, etc.; and further Or Zarua that that, etc.; and what we say at end of topics, etc.; and s.v. petachah, etc. but Rabbeinu Tam, etc.; but those stringencies of kohl, etc. and opened, etc.; and so Rosh end siman 38; and Semag in name of Aruch added on Tosafot words also R. Eliezer's matter and explained — certainly his words for purity matter for all learned from impurity and purity of negaim, etc. below for purity; and nevertheless Rosh wrote one should be stringent like Rashi's words and so Tosafot s.v. petachah there and so ruled anonymously in many places in this siman; and what is written here "some say" and not in those because his view is like Rif and Sha"s except Semag in name of Ra'em and Mordechai and Hagahot Maimoniyot wrote even per Rif and supporters specifically after the fact but l'chatchila even for her husband; therefore in scab and eye-mucus and kohl he wrote in Gemara language interposes; and for each as he holds l'chatchila or after the fact; but in kohl and filing even per Raavad counted after the fact as above siman 41 and 44 from fear and shame — if she knows herself she immersed well it is well; and so in Teshuvot HaRashba; but here he wrote "some say" per Raavad's view above and what is in siman 35 and if she changed, etc. is Semag and Aruch view but all poskim disagree as above; and this is "and some say" there and what is in siman 38 and if she jumped not, etc. is per "for it is mishnah" and as poskim distinguished between squeezing eyes and there; but what he ruled anonymously in disqualifying (seif 41) and put food (end siman 199) that did not count — his words are astounding and he followed Rambam and Tur in copying and did not mention above; and what is in siman 43 "some say she should not" is also per Rashi and likewise per Raavad except Raavad does not agree in explanation but explains like Tosafot and so explained all Geonim and poskim as above siman 41 except nevertheless he agreed in law as in his book brought by Beit Yosef who distinguished between immersing the bed and filing matter — for person is different entering between his fingers; and so Ran [and so in Teshuvot HaRashba and further "some say" likewise for person and that here is mud on riverbank; and possibly this is Rashi's view in Niddah "they pass over them" and above siman 41 and what is stated]. And so Rosh in siman 43 — and some distinguish person from vessels, etc. except he disagreed and wrote — however per reason water precedes no distinction and agreed to Tosafot's explanation and therefore wrote there "some say," etc. (end).`,
    '40#ג': `(Extract) Or ties loose garment, etc. As I wrote siman 46 — woman who immersed, etc. (end).`,
    '43#_': `(Extract) Some say, etc. As stated Megillah 1 — Rava asked: zavah whose teeth fell out, etc. Sha"d; and in hagahah there wrote no proof from there for question there is because of zavah's springs and for purity; and if because she must hold and water entry not fit there is no proof except from compressing lips; and nevertheless from his words it seems he agrees in law — doubtful against that Gemara proves opposite specifically for purity as above (end).`,
    '48#ב': `Such as, etc. Like R. Natan whom we hold like him as I wrote section 1 — halachah is like R. Natan.`,
    '48#ג': `Or, etc. This is all the more — even per Rabbis as I wrote (section 2 section 2).`,
    '48#ד': `And there are, etc. Hagahot Ashri there and Sha"s — Rav and R. Yehudah halachah like R. Yehudah; and further in Torah law follow the stringent.`,
    '48#ה': `There are those who wrote, etc. As stated end of chapter 5 of Eruvin (55b) and Hagahot Ashri there s.v. yoshvei, etc.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 198 in Taz siman 21) And his words are astounding, etc. See in Siftei Kohen small seif 25 — nothing is difficult. Also what was told him that a great one answered in responsum like his words — that great one is Maharam of Lublin and I already challenged him in Siftei Kohen there see there. Also what he ruled on Shabbat she immerses again and is forbidden to cut nail and concluded "I saw one who erred and commanded cutting on Yom Tov through gentile maidservant," etc. — he ruled well and as I will explain. For what he wrote that since assisting is as if doing melachah forbidden on Shabbat and Yom Tov like circumciser above siman 181 — he erred in two ways. One — even cutting with utensil he himself is exempt here Torah prohibition per most poskim and all the more taking in hand per all there is no Torah prohibition as I will explain; and further here assisting does not apply for only in circumcision where one circumcises and one is circumcised is implied as Rashi explained in These are lashed 84b — we learn Scripture thus "do not circumcise" do not let circumcise — or from plural language after Divine warning circumciser and circumcised until here his language; but in other warnings cannot say liable for assisting for assisting has no substance; and so explicitly in chapter HaMetzni'a (Shabbat 93) and chapter 2 of Beitzah 22 — assisting has no substance for Shabbat and permitted even l'chatchila and agreed by all poskim see there; and if so since it implies from poskim in any case there is mitzvah in taking nails as I wrote and telling gentile is rabbinic prohibition and in place of mitzvah they did not decree, etc. — all the more per Raavad and his colleagues I brought in Siftei Kohen siman 25 that nail prevents by law plainly it is place of mitzvah and permitted to tell gentile for even if he himself takes nails there is no Torah prohibition for in chapter HaMetzni'a we say per all one who takes nails for another there is only rabbinic prohibition without utensil and per Rabbis even for himself only rabbinic prohibition; and so wrote poskim and as explained in Tur and Shulchan Aruch Orach Chayyim siman 328 seif 41; and what they wrote there if did not specify majority by hand exempt but forbidden with utensil liable for chatat — likewise did not specify at all by hand exempt as evident in Sha"s except because they stated first "specified majority permitted l'chatchila" they stated also at end "did not specify majority" — or come to teach even if specified partially there is still prohibition and with utensil liable for chatat; and if so properly one may tell gentile to take nail by hands or teeth since it is place of mitzvah; and even with utensil would appear permitted for Tosafot wrote chapter HaMetzni'a that mishnah comes per R. Yehudah — if so Ramban and other poskim ruled like R. Shimon that melachah not needed for itself is exempt as explained in Ran chapter HaMetzni'a and so wrote Maggid Mishneh chapter 1 of Laws of Shabbat that view of Rashba and Acharonim halachah is like R. Shimon and so Tosafot in first chapter of Shabbat; and Tur wrote siman 316 and other vermin trapping not for need exempt — namely like R. Shimon as in Sha"s chapter Eight Vermin; and I wonder why he ruled siman 328 with utensil liable for chatat and also difficult on Shulchan Aruch for in siman 316 he brought Tur's view and Ramban's view and in siman 328 ruled anonymously with utensil liable for chatat; and strained to say they disagree with Tosafot who wrote for R. Shimon in nails exempt for Tosafot's words are clear and reason compels them; and more reasonable to say in siman 328 it does not deal with melachah not needed for itself and states law and practical difference where nails themselves are needed — if not so even with utensil exempt but forbidden; and also otherwise there are poskim who even complete melachah permit telling gentile in place of mitzvah as explained in Ran end of chapter R. Eliezer of circumcision and in hagahah of Shulchan Aruch Orach Chayyim siman 276; if so here is doubly advantageous; but since one can take without utensil why be lenient with utensil; but without utensil plainly permitted and no law requires six.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Sometimes. See in responsum Zichron Yosef part 14 siman 10 who wrote nevertheless we require specifically that she is particular at appointed times — like that Taz wrote siman 23 that tight ring on finger interposes because she removes it at time of kneading — behold if dough happens to her to knead several times today or tomorrow she removes it; but if she is not particular only once for long time — not; and what Shulchan Aruch wrote "even if she is not particular about it now," etc. — meaning only she is particular at end for distant time; rather meaning she is not particular now at time of immersion nevertheless she is particular on days before and after immersion see there; and see what I wrote siman 12 in his name.`,
    '3#_': `That interpose. See in responsum Penei Aryeh siman 12 — asked about woman who wears sponge in ear to draw out filth she forgot to remove sponge at time of immersion; and he wrote he did not know clear law on this — therefore she should immerse again without blessing see there.`,
    '4#_': `Interpose for she is particular about them. [Wrote in Levushi Sharad letter 196 — and although water enters them and what harm since she is particular to remove them already Beit Yosef wrote Tur ruled like Rashi's teachers that those laws of seif 1 majority if particular interposes deal even when water enters, etc.; per this those laws of seifim 3–4 that do not interpose deal specifically in minority but when covering majority of hairs even if not particular and water enters nevertheless interposes; and per this somewhat difficult that of seif 5 explained with two hairs or more does not interpose even if particular or majority because not tight — must say specifically in matter not of her body like seifim 3–4 we say if particular or majority interposes even if water enters unlike hairs themselves which are her body — no room to say interposes unless water does not enter. But all this per Beit Yosef; however Darkei Moshe and Perishah disagree that Rashi's view is primary and law of gilded ones reason is not interposition but trembling and immersion did not count well see there; and per their words seifim 3–4 do not interpose even in majority since water enters. And it appears primary like Darkei Moshe and Perishah not like Beit Yosef for so proven from Mordechai on scab of ringworm, etc.; and further proof from law of seif 23 ornaments and earrings, etc.; and further clear proof from seif 40 or ties loose garment, etc. (and further clear proof from seif 46 woman who immersed in her garments permitted even though majority and possibly particular — astonishing in my eyes Rav did not mention this) — therefore primary like Darkei Moshe and Perishah and so we rule halachah wherever water enters even majority and particular permitted. And by law even l'chatchila she immerses with them and we do not decree loose because of not loose except where we find such as friend holds her and likewise ear earrings since troublesome for her to remove; but elsewhere we do not decree; however since some are stringent with all loose to remove — l'chatchila practice stringency with all and remove; but if already immersed certainly do not be stringent at all and not like Beit Yosef (brought below siman 10 see there); and he wrote further since I proved reason of gilded is only trembling — if so one may say only l'chatchila as explained seif 41 reason of trembling does not invalidate immersion after the fact, etc. — doubtful in law for halachah end quote see there. Again I saw in Taz siman 13 he also extended on this and there challenged also from seif 46 mentioned — but in other language he did not mention there it is majority only that there she is particular and answered one may say it deals with inferior garments she is not particular on see there — appears his view even per Rashi's teachers forbidden where water enters only if particular but if not particular even in majority permitted; and therefore also left challenging from seif 40 mentioned; and in my humble opinion astonishing to say so for certainly no distinction between minority particular and majority not particular after Rashi's teachers hold halachah majority and particular was stated also in matter not tight and language interposes not precise — if so also what they decreed majority not particular because of majority particular is also in such case though not tight; and even per what he wishes in Taz there that he holds per this view Scripturally no interposition except in tight matter except Sages decreed, etc. — also no reason to distinguish minority particular from majority not particular (see Bach and understand) and concludes where there is further side to be lenient one may be lenient if they are loose see there; and see further in Tiferet Moshe on this].`,
    '5#_': `Stuck one to another. See in responsum Avnei Nezer part 2 siman 147 who wrote here it deals even if majority of hairs stuck; therefore Mordechai needed another reason and did not write reason because not particular; and he wrote further regarding woman who became ill and doctors said no remedy unless make merlakin and impossible unless powders like flour scattered on head whereby hairs tangle and when rinsed in water those powders cannot let hairs tangle and illness returns — doubtful if she can immerse so for first two Mordechai reasons apply only to those braids made from hairs themselves not this made by other thing; and also last two reasons doubtful in case before us — he ruled not to be lenient unless majority of her hairs remained not tangled then it is minority not particular and rabbinically we rely on expert doctor saying healing through this and it is rabbinic extension; but to do entire head or majority of her hair there is doubt of Torah prohibition in our hands to permit see there. [And see in Taz siman 19 they also brought and wrote his words correct for law and conclude in any case with all hairs or majority of her hairs certainly forbidden l'chatchila by head covering and powders and if she did so it interposes in immersion see there; and see in responsum Chasam Sofer part 6 siman 82 regarding woman wanting to stick hairs with wax for healing — plainly interposes and therefore they did not debate in responsum Penei Meivin and Taz rather by powders and head covering which themselves do not interpose only sticking of hairs interposes and on this they debated whether rabbinic extension; nevertheless they agreed to prohibition; but by wax wax itself interposes and cannot say rabbinic extension — plainly interposes and Heaven forbid to be lenient at all end quote].`,
    '8#_': `Woman with scabs. Taz siman 14 brought incident that wife of Maharar"k needed to stand before him naked, etc.; and although forbidden to look at covered places as above siman 195 seif 7 — already answered in responsum Noda Biyhudah second part 14 siman 122 see there in hagahah of author's son who wrote here it deals after immersion and so language — no sock on her and we are not concerned perhaps fell in water since so stuck it hurts to remove see there.`,
    '11#_': `She needs. Baer Heitev and what he wrote if it happened she forgot on Shabbat, etc. — see in responsum Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 8 who wrote it appears to him primary for practice like conclusion of Baal Nekudot HaKesef to take through gentile by hand or teeth — even though in responsum part 98 he challenged him — namely only to take with utensil but in such case he agrees; and Nekudot HaKesef himself for practice did not permit except by hand or teeth; and for benefit of matter wrote even with utensil possible to permit per some poskim but one should not rely in practice except by hand or teeth see there.`,
    '12#_': `Its minority interposes. [See in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 195 regarding brides who immerse with long hairs to be cut after mitzvah relations — one rabbi doubted concern they interpose since end to be revealed; and he wrote no concern here for what we say everywhere "all standing" means when about to be done immediately without interruption of other matter between them as Tosafot Bava Kamma 76; and here hairs do not stand to be shaved until after mitzvah relations and relations interrupt between immersion and shaving — not applicable "standing to be cut" like cut; and further clear proof from Nazir 59b see there].`,
    '13#_': `Loose. See in Baer Heitev of Rav Mahari"t who wrote in name of Beit Yosef interposes even after the fact [only if she already had relations after immersion with loose ring she needs no other immersion]; and see in responsum Makom Shmuel siman 55 who disagrees and ruled like Shulchan Aruch see there; and in writings of great Rav R. Daniel I saw he is very stringent even if one night passed after this immersion with loose ring one should be stringent requiring other immersion only if very loose may be lenient; and see in responsum Bach Chadashot siman 41 [who wrote Heaven forbid to say with loose immersion did not count and one who says so deserves rebuke and Rambam spoke only regarding holding person and vessels, etc. (see siman 28 Taz and Siftei Kohen); but rings we do not decree loose because of not loose by law except ear earrings since troublesome to remove. And all such where troublesome for her we decree from stringency l'chatchila; and likewise threads on neck hanging amulets, etc. see there; and also brought in Levushi Sharad letter 193 and see what I wrote siman 3 s.v. particular also in Taz siman 6 and Magen Gibborim his view only l'chatchila should be careful but after the fact immersion counted see there].`,
    '14#_': `And if they are tight. [Baer Heitev and see in Sefer Sheyurei Taharah siman 44 who wrote from words of Gan HaMelech siman 123 clear woman whose way is not to knead herself — ring on her hand does not interpose even very tight; and similarly wrote in Agur in Orach Chayyim siman 161 in name of Rashal ring on men does not interpose because they are not particular except ring with precious stone which is also from way for men do not do such work; nevertheless do not be lenient since in Shulchan Aruch here and Orach Chayyim wrote anonymously tight ring interposes and did not distinguish — and one may say for sellers of dye and dyers all that craft known thus unlike here one may say no distinction; however possibly after the fact if she had relations may be lenient in this case see there].`,
    '15#_': `And likewise law of bandage. See in responsum Shivat Tziyon siman 42 — woman with eye condition and expert doctor commanded no water at all on eyes even outside eyes lest she lose eyesight — how should she conduct chafifah and immersion; questioner thought to provide loose bandage on eyes at immersion time; and although Bach wrote in name of Sha"d regarding ornaments and earrings we decree l'chatchila loose because of not loose and so Rama above seif 1 in hagahah specifically ornaments and earrings always on her, etc. — Rav author does not agree to this remedy; rather even one who holds ornaments and earrings we do not decree because of not loose — there is no benefit to woman if immerses tight; unlike here where main purpose of bandage is to guard water from eyes — concern she will tighten from fear; but there is another remedy — she places hands on eyes and rinses hands first until wet enough to wet another as below seif 28; but placing hands should not be by woman herself for if she presses hands upward wrinkles form and flesh in joint of hand and arm becomes covered as above seif 35 immersion must be as she walks — only another woman descends with woman into mikveh and stands behind her at slight distance not touching body of immersing woman and rinses hands in water and covers eyes of this woman loosely and she immerses; and for chafifah he ruled leniently — sufficient she wash and scrub entire body and head except eyes and suffices careful looking in mirror or examination by another woman that there be no eye-mucus and eyelashes not stuck see there; and see in responsum Maharam Ziskind of Rothenburg siman 18.`,
    '16#_': `Hidden place. See in responsum Noda Biyhudah part Yoreh De'ah siman 64 — woman who had wax ring placed deep in vestibule for healing whether interposes in immersion; and he wrote this is certainly called particular for constantly soiled in niddah days and must remove when she stops for purity to clean and likewise in childbirth days and perhaps also impedes bedikah always in seven clean days and must remove — if so it is matter she is particular about and interposes; however although in Torat Kohanim must be fit for water entry specifically in hidden place they said so but in place called throat need not even fit for water entry; therefore if this woman says it is so deep sun does not reach even at completion of entry then called throat and need not be fit for water entry and need not remove at immersion see there — he ruled so for law not for practice until two more rabbis agree [and in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 152 disagrees with beginning of Noda Biyhudah's words what he wrote called particular because must remove at time of stopping purity and likewise childbirth days — wrote that removal at childbirth is not called particular for that removal is not from concern of particular neither on ring nor body filth but to make room for child; and proof from man's ring without stone not interposing as Magen Avraham siman 161 wrote though removed at tefillin and lulav — only if needed remove at separation of purity this is certainly particular; and what Noda Biyhudah wrote if this woman says so deep, etc. — he added siman 193 even if doubtful and possibly doubt of lack of knowledge expert woman can clarify — nevertheless since danger and great weakness to remove it is called minority and not particular, etc. — may be lenient see there]. And see in Noda Biyhudah second part Yoreh De'ah siman 135 — one rabbi wanted to divide hidden place itself — where water can enter by themselves like mouth if open and nostrils and ear holes except Scripture revealed those need not water entry but still need fit for water entry; but woman's place even outer part since even if she spreads legs water enters only slightly at beginning of place — therefore all inward from where infant sits and is visible need not even fit for water entry; and he rejected his proofs (also from Rama's words seif 43 in hagahah clear not like him) see there he ruled for bedikah of clean days if arrangement that mouth of womb faces cavity of ring and entire mouth of source also faces cavity of ring — then at separation of purity she places cotton within clean linen somewhat thick at least width of cavity of ring and inserts this witness deep through cavity of ring until opposite mouth of source and she has beforehand hefsek taharah until after minchah; and also day 1 she does so and other six days she examines as possible see there at length; and see in responsum Zichron Yosef part 14 siman 10 who also agrees to above Noda Biyhudah; also brought from responsum Shevut Yaakov part 1 siman 69 who explained for minority particular we say safek derabbanan lekula (requires study now for in established prohibition we do not say safek derabbanan as above siman 110 laws of stains letter 20 and here established prohibition of niddah tumah — must say he holds like Peri Chadash I brought there and I already wrote there halachah is not so and now safek hefsek log before me) and further because woman said cannot take ring from there due to danger of life except at childbirth pains necessarily takes lest fetus be blocked — therefore called not particular (see what I wrote siman 1 in his name); and for bedikah he ruled she can examine herself like other women with cloth inserting to middle of vestibule near where sun treads and leave there entire twilight at separation for purity and likewise one of counting days morning or evening and also examine left side of womb and though right side and ring place cannot examine — no matter only do what possible to examine all places reachable and other days light bedikah suffices see there at length; and see in responsum Rabbi Akiva Eiger siman 60 who greatly extended on this matter and agreed to leniency for interposition and also Gaon of Lissa agreed to permission; however in what he was lenient in Zichron Yosef responsum for bedikah he disagrees since many of our rabbis who are stringent require specifically examination of holes and cracks and implies not examination at all without hidden places; however one thing — there is room to debate and be lenient — one may say what requires examination of hidden places is since per majority cannot insert witness deep until place sun actually treads as Beit Yosef wrote and even at completion of setting sun not exactly at source — concerned zav goes sideways to hidden places and does not meet witness — therefore remedy for our sister to examine with tube (thereby can push cloth more) and cloth inside and push cloth much until enters inward from where sun treads near actual am — this is proof if source zav would drip on cloth and does not require examination of hidden places — so will be bedikah of stopping purity and day 1 of seven clean days; and he concluded may be lenient by above bedikah if great ones of generation agree and even if very lenient only bedikah of stopping purity as above and rely on Ramban that bedikah of seven clean days does not require hidden places — also agrees see there; and see in responsum Bashamayim Rosh siman 156 in hagahah of Kessef David what he extended on this both for bedikah and interposition see there; and see further in She'eilot Uteshuvot Teshuvah Me'ahavah part 1 siman 36 through siman 42 [and in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 192 and 193] what he wrote on this.`,
    '19#_': `Planks. See in Baer Heitev in name of Maharam of Lublin; and in Taz who wrote where stones not found possible to fix with planks each not so wide fit for foot treading except by joining many planks then not fit for treading before joining to mikveh, etc. see there further. (And see in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 201 regarding mikveh floor of brick building they placed thick planks so water not seep between bricks and planks interwoven woman to woman by iron and teacher commanded making holes like spout of skin-bottle to fulfill words in Shulchan Aruch and Maharam Lublin — and he wrote to him not correct and if wants stringency of Maharam Lublin what did holes help — are they not fit for treading for holes help nullify vessel-receiving category but not treading category; rather now worse since on stone floor holes made for receiving and are actual vessel (in my humble opinion not understood per his own words siman 206 brought somewhat below siman 201 siman 18 see there); and further concern water entering holes drawn below between planks and bricks perhaps make crawling and disqualify entire mikveh by law as Shulchan Aruch siman 201 seif 50 (also not fully understood see Shulchan Aruch there seif 51 and what he wrote there in his name siman 211 and possibly resolved); and further I wonder if wants stringency of Maharam Lublin how fix with iron which receives tumah and thereby wood also receives tumah because iron supports them, etc. (also difficult per his own words siman 206 brought somewhat below siman 201 siman 28) — therefore appears certainly one wanting stringency should make stone floor without planks at all and lenient did not lose even with irons since made initially for building; but at least should seal holes commanded because of crawling concern which is Torah concern end quote see there); and see in responsum Noda Biyhudah second part Yoreh De'ah siman 138 — mikveh deep and women afraid to descend to immerse — they took chair and placed in mikveh tied with ropes to stand on chair while immersing — one should rebuke them; and although per one reasoning Taz relied for permission of planks one can find permission here too — nevertheless do not add for there are several reasoning to permit unlike bench explicit in mishnah chapter 8 of Mikvaot forbidden — do not rely on external reasoning to permit see there.`,
    '24#_': `Who immersed in her garments. [Baer Heitev and see what I wrote siman 3 s.v. particular about them].`,
    '27#_': `Permitted to her husband. See what challenged on this in Sefer Yom Teru'ah page 8a; and see in responsum Radbaz Chadashot siman 34 who resolved his difficulty see there further; and see in Magen Avraham siman 158 siman 13 who wrote here blessing does not apply.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '3#_': `(Siftei Kohen siman 13) And who compelled him to this. Beit Yosef's words are astounding for since Ri'az holds it does not refer to eye-mucus — if so what he wrote regarding dry outside eye perforce he holds like Rosh that Mar Ukva refers to outside eye as Beit Yosef wrote s.v. from outside eye, etc. and so from Ri'az's words, etc. — if so what did Beit Yosef challenge "who compelled him" — behold Mar Ukva innovated leniency of outside eye that mishnah teaches interposes specifically dry — and how is it applicable it refers only to purity?`,
    '5#_': `(there siman 17) And difficult for if so even per Rabbi it does not fit. I already wrote in margin of Beit Yosef — Beit Yosef holds one may say that Tosefta stating even on wood it does not interpose — or refers to Rabbi who distinguishes wood from metals and Sages hold in every case it does not interpose; and with this Beit Yosef's words fit well and so I found in Maharshak.`,
    '6#_': `(seif 23) Ornaments and earrings. See responsum Tashbetz (part 3 end) who proved specifically nose rings interpose but ear rings do not interpose; and see in Bach (part 2 siman 61) who wrote opposite — ear rings even loose interpose.`,
    '8#_': `(Siftei Kohen siman 32) And in vain he strained. And especially if we say the question needs proof — if so in siman 26 at least there is no proof to be lenient for in truth interposition was found; and even per Siftei Kohen's conclusion here therefore it depends to be lenient implying in any case examination of hidden place is needed except mishnah speaks of examination after immersion and we are not concerned it fell on ascent for we rely to be lenient; and also difficult from that of siman 26 for there is no examination of hidden place at all for in truth interposition was found except we can attribute to preoccupation — nevertheless there was no examination of hidden place — doubtful.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '2#ב': `Katla — these three words in Ashkenazi pronunciation.`,
    '7#ב': `And if it is dry it interposes, etc. View of Raavad, Rashba, Rambam, Rabbeinu Tam, Semag and several other Geonim — this interposition and interposition of seifim 8–9 is only for purity but not for her husband; and Beit Yosef brings them — and it is astonishing he did not mention their view here; and further rabbinic matter for Scripturally interposition is only majority and particular and in rabbinic matters follow the lenient; and so appears view of Rabbeinu Yerucham who wrote dry eye-mucus interposes — this is outside eye from when it begins to turn green like blue in eye it does not interpose even on eye; and although in Tosefta it states interposes and so in Sha"s — specifically for purity but not for husband and so Rambam and so primary and so agreed most poskim as we learn "and halachah is not like all these topics," etc. — end quote; and Beit Yosef wrote on him — appears he holds what is said "halachah is not like all these topics" does not refer to eye-mucus in eye, etc. — I do not know who compelled him, etc. — but not so for it refers to eye-mucus in eye; and what he wrote dry eye-mucus outside eye interposes — all agree on this because Mar Ukva does not deal with this and plainly interposes per all; and so Beit Yosef himself at end s.v. and Raavad wrote regarding scab, etc. — however all this in eye-mucus inside eye — if outside eye Mar Ukva does not deal with it and even for husband interposes per law per above explanations — end quote; and what Rabbeinu Yerucham wrote dry specifically follows Semag and Mordechai view see there; nevertheless appears Rabbeinu Yerucham holds inside eye even dry does not interpose and so Agudah and other poskim brought view of these Geonim who permit for husband; however Ra'em and Semag and Mordechai wrote even per those who say specifically for purity — nevertheless l'chatchila for husband must be careful in everything and so implied in several places for husband l'chatchila is like purity (and plain l'chatchila must be careful as Rav wrote above seif 1); and possibly also Rosh who wrote correct to be stringent like Rashi — l'chatchila he speaks; nevertheless appears where she can immerse again she should be stringent but where impossible certainly worthy to rely on all those great ones who ruled for purity specifically was stated not for husband; and Mechaber himself brought their reasoning below siman 44 and Taz see there; and see Beit Yosef.`,
    '11#ב': `It does not interpose. And if flesh membrane is on it in every case it does not interpose — end quote Tur's language (meaning even visible under skin); and it appears per Tosefta explanation regarding visible as Semag, Semak, and Mordechai explained and also Rambam, Rosh, and Rabbeinu Yerucham — it appears they explained so; and Beit Yosef wrote he holds Tosefta deals with not visible and mishnah follows Rabbi who is individual and nevertheless does not distinguish metal from wood — difficult for if so mishnah does not follow even Rabbi for Rabbi admits wood interposes and mishnah does not distinguish wood or metal (and likewise above Beit Yosef wrote impossible to explain Tosefta as not visible externally for then mishnah stands like individual for it does not distinguish metals from wood — namely like Rabbi who in all does not interpose when not visible externally — end quote and this also not correct for Rabbi too wood interposes — if so mishnah does not follow Rabbi either); and further if so Rambam, Rosh, and Rabbeinu Yerucham should have distinguished and written wood even not visible interposes — rather certainly they hold mishnah follows Sages and Tosefta deals with visible — therefore no distinction wood or metals except visible even metal interposes like Sages of Tosefta and not visible even wood does not interpose like mishnah which does not distinguish; and therefore poskim did not mention distinction wood or metal only visible or not visible; and what Rambam, Rosh, and Rabbeinu Yerucham did not write flesh membrane permitted — because plain Tosefta did not need to say so except because it deals with visible and does not teach not visible does not interpose; but if not visible externally does not interpose plainly all the more flesh membrane does not interpose (and also in Beit Yosef itself proven flesh membrane is better than not visible for he explained Tosefta deals with not visible — if so he holds Sages interpose; and nevertheless teaches at end flesh membrane s.v. does not interpose) (or possibly they explain "not visible" of mishnah — namely flesh membrane covers it) — all this not like Beit Yosef; and on contrary difficult for Beit Yosef why they did not mention membrane to teach even wood which interposes per Rabbi does not interpose with membrane as in Tosefta — all this appears clear to me; again I found in Darkei Moshe who wrote Beit Yosef's words are garbled here — if so Rosh and supporters should distinguish metal from wood — end quote.`,
    '16#_': `Blood that flowed, etc. — beginning to dry and stick somewhat that when finger inserted a thread is drawn from it — Rashi in Menachot (21a); and implies main distinction is flowed or not but no distinction flowed in flesh or in something else; and so in Sha"s there answers this flowed this did not flow see there; and in Behag (88a) I found Rav said — this Sages said moist blood does not interpose — they said only when not cooked; but if cooked by fire whether moist or dry interposes — end quote; and although not found so in our Sha"s and from not answering so in Menachot does not imply — nevertheless Behag's words are words of tradition.`,
    '20#_': `She needs another immersion. In truth this stringency is not found in any posek; and on contrary from mishnah and many poskim and Taz appears clear whatever nail is clean certainly does not interpose; only Hagahot Sha"d wrote good to be stringent and immerse again because impossible there not be mud inside; and therefore Rav wrote they practice stringency; and Maharam of Lublin wrote responsum siman 81 — since this is not from law but stringency appears they were stringent only when found she forgot one nail immediately after immersion before sleeping with husband; but if not found until tomorrow not proper to be stringent lest cast aspersion on her relations and even if did not have relations that night matter unseemly and needs no other immersion if no filth found under it — end quote; however in Raavad siman 326 page 60d I found she must cut fingernails and toenails for since she will take them they interpose now — end quote; and this resembles what Rav wrote and since already practiced taking nails, etc. needs other immersion, etc. — implies reason is not because impossible mud remained under nail but nail itself interposes; and so Bach seif 19 — appears even if certain no mud at all should be stringent she immerse again for there is opinion if nail stands to be cut it interposes — end quote; and if so doubtful in this ruling Maharam of Lublin ruled; therefore appears where she can return and immerse even if not found until tomorrow she should return and immerse and it seems to me so is practiced to rule; but where impossible do not be stringent since night passed; and see above siman 197 siman 1.`,
    '24#א': `That if she immersed, etc. Bach wrote from this language implies not obligation on her but she needs concern for her benefit that if she immersed and something found between them immersion did not count; and similarly wrote Mahar"i in Teshuvot HaRashba regarding shechitah (above siman 18 siman 17) and built his foundation on this precision — end quote; and not clear for specifically above regarding shechitah we say one who slaughters many animals must check between each — if not done and checked last and knife found defective behold all doubt nevelot — therefore Mahar"i precisely inferred from this language — if prohibition were from another reason lest eat before final check would not say so — see there explained explicitly; but here apparently obligation is on her — namely reason because if she immersed and something found between them immersion did not count; therefore we are concerned perhaps interposing matter between them for she need not check after immersion if interposing matter on her; but in shechitah knife must be checked after end of slaughter; and so implied in Beit Yosef obligation is on her; and he wrote law is so for teeth commonly have food remnants between them; and thereby settled what Bach challenged from where obligation — so is law and understand; and also in Taz wrote and if vav "even if she said I will check after immersion between teeth" we are concerned perhaps she forgets to check; and similarly we are concerned in good places; and one may say for shechitah there anyway must check knife after slaughter unlike here.`,
    '26#_': `We rely to be lenient, etc. Rokeach's view is not so who wrote siman 319 — I found incident with woman who immersed and meat found between teeth and they required another immersion; from that incident brought first chapter of Kiddushin incident with Rabbi's maidservant who immersed and bone found between teeth and Rabbi required another immersion; and even if we do not know when it entered between teeth (before immersion or) after immersion — we say she immersed and counted and interposing matter found on her even though occupied with that type all day immersion did not count — end quote; and one should be stringent.`,
    '28#א': `Unless she loosened, etc. Bach ruled like Rambam and Ramban that even if loosened immersion did not count — decree because of not loosened; and although generally we do not decree loose because of not loose here is different because from being hasty and afraid lest slip from hands he presses hands on it unintentionally — end quote; and so Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit 157b — here regarding holding companion whom companion leans on him he is accustomed and likely not to loosen — properly can decree — end quote; however in Behag chapter 5 page 2 girsa R. Shimon says until he loosens, etc. — namely like Rashba's explanation he argues on end section — examine.`,
    '31#ג': `She transgressed and immersed on these, etc. Bach disagreed — appears not to be lenient since per Rashi and Tosafot immersion did not count even in kohl from bathhouse decree, etc.; and his words astonish me for Rashi and Tosafot did not write so except per Rav Kahana's view but in conclusion implied in Sha"s no bathhouse decree in kohl and as all poskim wrote; and further one may say even bathhouse decree is only l'chatchila and as I wrote siman 201 siman 37 per Rosh and Tur; and even per poskim who hold even after the fact bathhouse decree applies — that is in vessel receiving tumah but in earthenware vessel one may say even per conclusion of Sha"s forbidden only l'chatchila — understand.`,
    '31#ה': `That has beams, etc. In Taz he greatly extended and protested mikvaot in these countries making planks below and steps to stand on at immersion see there; and already one hundred Akiva's challenged him in Ma'adanei Melech 317a and hagahah of Darkei Moshe and Bach and Baal Magdol David; and Maharam of Padua already extended in responsum siman 31 and raised permission reason because plain wooden vessels per many poskim do not receive tumah even rabbinically; and even Rashba (and Mechaber) deal only when this ladder was in beams before they fixed it in mikveh for initially vessel name was on them and afterward fixed in mikveh; but if initially made planks or steps in mikveh to stand at immersion no vessel name on them only building name called on them and permitted to immerse on them; and so Bach and Magdol David see there extended (however what Magdol David challenged there — not correct and understand); also in responsum She'erit Yosef siman 60 wrote Rashba (and Mechaber) deal with planks and beams made for treading but when made only to stand in water and immerse they are not fit to receive treading tumah therefore permitted to stand there at immersion — end quote; and so appears compelled Mechaber's view from him and in him that he wrote in following seif if they have no holes permitted to stand on it — what difference they have no holes — behold Rosh in responsum wrote only since they have no holes and are plain wooden vessels not receiving tumah permitted; and Beit Yosef brings; and if so likewise with spouts even though no holes should be forbidden (and what Taz wrote regarding holes does not appear and understand) — rather certainly as we said; and for this permitted with spouts because initially no vessel name on them for not fit for treading only plain wood for mikveh need; and so wrote Beit HaEzer — appears even per Raavad and Rashba who disqualify plain wood fixed in ground there with planks fit for treading; but spouts not fit for treading permitted — end quote; per this what Mechaber wrote here immersion did not count — they are plain vessels before fixed in mikveh — examine; and see (in following note there is further side of permission — examine); and Maharam of Lublin responsum siman 57 wrote certainly do not change custom lest cast aspersion on early ones; nevertheless whenever before me they repair or build mikveh I always command fixing with things not fit for treading so valid per all — end quote see there; and see above siman 201 seif 12.`,
    '32#_': `If he has no hole, etc. Tur did not mention this condition — because he holds Rosh in responsum permitted from two reasons — one we do not say regarding spring that passed over bench for there it deals spring passed to mikveh over bench and R. Shimon explained they decreed on fixed vessels, etc. until this decree does not apply here; and further plain spouts have no holes, etc. — end quote; and Tur holds first answer primary as language implies and second answer wrote only for benefit of matter and therefore even if they have holes permitted; however this is specifically per R. Shimon and Rosh view; but Raavad, Rashba, and Ran who forbade even detached vessels placed for mikveh — if so not permitted here from first reason; and therefore Mechaber permitted because no holes — understand.`,
    '35#ב': `Immersion counted for her. Doubtful on this; and likewise squeezing eyes excessively below siman 39 — some say immersion counted — Mechaber above seif 8 regarding kohl and seif 9 blood in wound interposes and this is only per those who do not have girsa in Sha"s for these depend on each other as evident in all poskim and Beit Yosef see there — and Taz left unexplained.`,
    '37#_': `It is well. Language of Behag page 25b — and in little water to immerse in it entire woman's height is bent like a fish and it is well; and so wrote other poskim.`,
    '39#_': `Some say, etc. In Ma'adanei Melech 318a wondered what is different compressing mouth that anonymously ruled immersion did not count — no difficulty for even disputant agrees compressing lips is mishnah as in all poskim; and distinction Ran wrote — what is different compressing lips that mishnah teaches as if she did not immerse — one may say when squeezing eyes wrinkles so much they prevent water entry — end quote; and Beit Yosef brings above regarding scab on wound 221b; and so Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit 129a — squeezing eyes excessively or opening excessively no concern except compressing lips alone — end quote; and so explained in other poskim.`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#ג': `Since way of majority of people, etc. Beit Yosef was uncertain if other people are not particular and she is particular; and wrote in Darkei Moshe Mordechai wrote explicitly interposes; and so wrote Mahari Chagiz.`,
    '1#ד': `And if it covers majority of body, etc. Scripturally interposition is only majority and particular; and Sages decreed on majority not particular because of majority particular; and decreed on minority particular also because of majority particular; but in minority not particular — two advantages they did not decree at all for it would be decree on decree. And what Rama afterward wrote decree because of interposing matters — meaning even in minority — there speaks l'chatchila but after the fact we do not decree decree on decree.`,
    '5#א': `Two hairs, etc. In Tur this law appears twice in this siman — once here and second where he wrote majority of hair tied strand, etc.; and his intent we find this law twice in Gemara — once in chapter What woman all agree regarding hair that does not interpose — meaning if she wound head hair with other hair does not interpose because she does not tighten well; and second in last chapter of Niddah Beit Yosef brings one strand interposes two I do not know three do not interpose — that deals with woman's own hairs if majority tied one by one then interposes specifically; and from this Tur below deals; and here deals adhesion of other hair to her hair; and practical difference between those two — here any interposition even minority is interposition like wool threads seif 2 and as I wrote there; therefore Tur did not write here need majority of her hair as below; but below deals with own hairs tied — need specifically majority for otherwise not particular; and Beit Yosef wrote on Tur's words here deals with very particular, etc. — not precise for this applies only below for reason I mentioned; but certainly regarding number they are equal — as with own hair depends how hairs tied one by one or two by two — likewise if wound one hair on her hair it interposes even if wound many hairs around this not in that place — in each winding one hair because tight; but if in each winding took two hairs or more no interposition; and this Tur wrote here in alternative when of two hairs — meaning each winding as I wrote; and these words also fit Ran Beit Yosef brought — "they agree regarding hair threads do not interpose" — meaning specifically on girls' heads hair does not interpose because cannot tighten well; but to tie one strand alone or strand with companion they tighten for we say in last chapter of Niddah one tied strand interposes, etc.; and even to wind those hair threads in her hairs also permitted because impossible those hair threads be more than one strand, etc. — end quote; behold he also wrote first law deals with winding around her hair and tying one strand deals with her own hair; and what he wrote "or one strand with companion" — also called one strand whether alone or with companion as Beit Yosef afterward wrote; and properly ruled like R. Yochanan Beit Yosef brings; and in vain Bach wrote here ruled like Rava — rather Ran concluded here in winding other hair no interposition because they do not make this winding in one strand; and Tur wrote if made from one hair it is interposition and no law distinction between them except factual circumstance; and per this Shulchan Aruch's words explained — what seif 4 wrote hair threads do not interpose means winding law we mentioned and there too distinction how winding made one hair or more; and there interposition if made from one hair even not majority and not particular for like wool threads seif 2 water does not enter until loosened; and here seif 5 deals with second law — her own hairs and depends on particular as Shulchan Aruch concluded here.`,
    '17#_': `That majority of craftspeople, etc. Beit Yosef in this siman wrote in name of Rokeach — appears if woman touched pot or kettle and flesh swelled slightly this is not particular even though little stuck to flesh; and from this reason I permitted woman who immersed and found blackness in place she touched bathhouse walls black from bath smoke — she needs no second immersion for similar to this we mentioned and it is a fortiori for hands need be cleaner more than body for hands always exposed and she is more particular about their cleanliness more than body; and unlike ink that interposes as Rokeach beginning seif 15 for there actual ink substance interposes unlike this blackness which is mere filth; and similarly Beit Yosef wrote in name of Rashba to distinguish dye from ink as above.`,
    '18#א': `Excrement under nail, etc. Tur concluded after this — however way of people is not to be particular about this therefore one not particular does not interpose — end quote; and Beit Yosef wrote refers also to dough; and Beit Yosef wondered how he ruled to distinguish when mishnah did not distinguish; and therefore did not bring here in Shulchan Aruch; and Rama in Darkei Moshe explained Tur deals with very minute matter people are not particular about; and as Semag and Semak wrote in name of Rabbeinu Tam — even excrement and dough if so minute minority people not particular about — minority not particular does not interpose — end quote.`,
    '18#ב': `Not against flesh interposes. Beit Yosef wrote in name of Semag in name of Rabbeinu Tam — this specifically mud similar to dough sticking greatly like potter's clay; but not other mud and excrement; and know for we learn in Mikvaot these do not interpose — filth on his flesh and excrement under nail — end quote; and did not mention this here in Shulchan Aruch to be so lenient; nevertheless appears to me to resolve what was challenged to Rema in Levush why not careful with this in hand-washing; and per what I wrote it fits well — certainly for hand immersion we rely on Rabbeinu Tam it does not interpose except potter's clay and similar.`,
    '20#ב': `And since already practiced taking, etc. In hagahah of Sha'arei Dura wrote in this language — and if she forgot and did not take nails before immersion nail does not interpose provided no mud inside; nevertheless good to be stringent and immerse second time because impossible no mud inside — end quote; and Rashal copied from Maharam's responsum on this that immersion counted all she checks under nails before immersion and no mud and excrement; and Mahari Chagiz wrote appears to him even if certain no excrement she should immerse again for there is opinion if nail stands to be cut it interposes, etc.; and his words astounding for all poskim wrote here only common practice to take nails and not from law; and appears all we find she needs second immersion for forgetting nail — only while she did not have relations with husband before remembering; but if after relations do not be stringent requiring second immersion since only stringency in world; and even if unknown no mud and excrement — implied from Hagahot Sha"d and Maharam's responsum above she needs second immersion by law only while did not have relations with husband but not afterward for even if excrement were there Scripturally does not interpose for majority and particular required — only rabbinic decree on minority particular as beginning of siman; if so here rabbinic doubt; and all the more per seif 18 in name of Semag and Rabbeinu Tam — specifically sticking mud required though not ruled in Shulchan Aruch for law; nevertheless here she already had relations and possibly conceived — if you come to be stringent requiring second immersion you cast aspersion on that child as we find first chapter of Gittin in this language "if you come to be stringent you cast aspersion on her children" — so appears clear in my humble opinion; and again I was told great one answered in responsum as I wrote; and since clarified above taking woman's nails is only from custom they were stringent on themselves — appears what is in Mordechai Hilchos Niddah and Hagahot Sha'arei Dura — if immersion occurred on chol hamoed proper that gentile maidservant shave them for telling gentile is rabbinic prohibition and in place of mitzvah they did not decree; and if no gentile maidservant scrape well under nail — end quote — specifically chol hamoed; but Shabbat and Yom Tov if forgot to take nails she should not tell gentile maidservant to cut for since no absolute mitzvah in taking nails for possible scraping under them alone — why nullify rabbinic Shabbat prohibition of telling gentile; and further it is stringency coming to leniency for even though gentile maidservant cuts nevertheless that Israelite woman assists by lowering hand as if she does this work forbidden Scripturally on Shabbat and Yom Tov like case we find siman 181 regarding circumcision border — circumciser also liable if assisted by leaning toward him to circumcise, etc.; and impossible to guard from this when letting gentile maidservant cut nails without Israelite lowering hand to her; and one may say not so that she should not immerse when this happens Shabbat and Yom Tov — for also on chol hamoed they permitted by scraping nails — why reduce Shabbat and Yom Tov from where no gentile maidservant on chol hamoed; rather clear matter on Shabbat and Yom Tov do not cut through gentile maidservant but scrape well and immerse; and Mordechai did not be stringent to cut through gentile maidservant except on chol hamoed where they were lenient even for her to cut herself; and so we hold Orach Chayyim siman 532 obligatory immersion per all as Rama wrote there; except Mordechai disciple of Maharam who was stringent forbidden to cut nails on chol hamoed except through gentile maidservant as Beit Yosef wrote there — therefore needed cutting through gentile maidservant per his reasoning; and per what we hold siman 532 there to permit cutting chol hamoed without change and Shabbat and Yom Tov do not cut at all but scrape well — so appears clear; and I saw one who erred and commanded cutting on Yom Tov through gentile maidservant and did not intend correctly per law.`,
    '21#_': `Hanging nail. Beit Yosef extended on this — why is there interposition in minority more than majority; and thereby strained to explain deals when some interposing matter on that hanging part; and Mordechai wrote — its majority interposes specifically majority of majority like whole; but its minority does not interpose since stands to separate and already began separating, etc. — end quote; and nevertheless not yet settled why no interposition in minority still stuck when majority separated; and further what is different from hanging limb standing to be cut which is interposition even when majority separated; and Mahari Chagiz gave reason because needs craftsman to cut and I did not know his distinction for after all stands to be cut; and what appears in my humble opinion — certainly no interposition in what still stuck for never exposed there; and as Beit Yosef in his challenge; rather on separated part we say so — if majority remains and minority cracked — crack place is narrow and water cannot enter that crack unlike if majority broke open crack widened and water can enter and is fit for water entry; but in limb and hanging flesh even if majority broke cannot leave hanging — very painful — rather always holds to body as able — therefore not fit for water entry there as above to explain this matter in my humble opinion.`,
    '23#_': `Tight interpose. And one may say minority not particular — Raavad explained because particular to remove at kneading time and is like wool threads seif 2.`,
    '28#א': `Her friend should not hold her. It states in mishnah chapter 8 of Mikvaot — one who holds person and vessels and immerses them — impure; and if he rinsed hands in water — pure because moisture on hands connects to mikveh water and no interposition; R. Shimon says he should loosen hands so water enters; Rambam and Ramban explained Tanna Kamma is stricter than R. Shimon — for R. Shimon loose hands suffice and Tanna Kamma insufficient — therefore ruled need rinse hands first specifically; and I already wrote siman 120 seif 2 can rinse hands even in drawn water unlike Rama there; and per their explanation all who rinses hands first need not loosen hands at all even if tightens with force on person and vessel immersed — no concern since already soiled; for if not say so what helps rinsing hands — also after rinsing we decree perhaps will tighten with force; and further proof Rashba in long Teshuvot HaRashba challenged Rambam and Ramban explanation — in many places we do not decree like ornaments and earrings Tur mentioned nearby permitted with loosening; and Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit resolved specifically holding person and vessels to immerse — concern perhaps will hold with force lest fall; but elsewhere why decree gratuitously — if so we see if rinsed hands first no concern afterward even if tightens with force; and Rashba explained R. Shimon speaks stringently — Tanna Kamma holds rinsing alone suffices and R. Shimon holds since accustomed to press hands on person and vessel he immerses — no connection even in moisture touching; and one may say for feet of immerser on mikveh floor mentioned below seif 30 — there water precedes feet and still moisture touching connects to mikveh; but here R. Shimon holds way of holder of vessels to press hands on them lest fall and likewise holds companion and prevents moisture on hands being connection at all; and Tanna Kamma not concerned at all and moisture touching is connection; and halachah like Tanna Kamma — need not loosen; but if loosens plainly helps; and concludes even for Tanna Kamma nevertheless must not press much with force when holding to immerse — so written in Teshuvot HaRashba and Mishmeret HaBayit Rashba himself compiled and Tur agreed to Rashba; and therefore ruled here in Shulchan Aruch like them — suffices whether loosening or rinsing hands first; and Mahari Chagiz wondered why ruled not like Rambam and Ramban — no wonder for worthy to rely on Rashba and Tur especially rabbinic matter Scripturally interposition only majority and particular. And there is further dispute between Rambam and Ramban versus Rashba — for Rambam and Ramban I proved nearby even if holds with force does not interpose all who rinsed hands first; and necessarily weight of vessel must be — here reason "because water precedes" not specifically mikveh water but other water too; and so Teshuvot HaRashba explicitly as Beit Yosef brought — "because water precedes and further there is moisture touching," etc.; and therefore Tur did not write only since moist and did not state since mikveh water precedes her for he holds here like Rambam and Ramban we mentioned.`,
    '30#_': `Because water precedes. Meaning when she inserted feet there water already preceded and touched feet before reaching floor and that water connects to mikveh — so Beit Yosef in name of Rosh implies from this reason specifically mikveh water that preceded feet; but if feet already moist from drawn water — not; for otherwise should have said feet already moist as Tur actually this language; rather certainly qualification is since mikveh water themselves preceded; and per what Mordechai wrote above regarding weight in immersed vessel inside another vessel — what became soiled first in drawn water helps — fits well here since standing on floor is weight and connection through moisture touching nullified; therefore need mikveh water themselves make qualification; and per Rambam and Ramban above even holding with force does not interpose all who rinsed hands first — necessarily weight of vessel here reason "because water precedes" not specifically mikveh water but other water too; and so Teshuvot HaRashba explicitly as Beit Yosef brought.`,
    '31#א': `And not on planks, etc. In Gemara Rava said woman should not stand on earthenware vessel and immerse — Rav Kahana thought to say reason is bathhouse decree meaning their bathhouses sit on clay benches similar to earthenware and will say immerse on them — told him Rav Chanan of Nehardea — there reason is fear lest she fall and not immerse properly — silta too fear; and Beit Yosef in name of Raavad wrote even per conclusion reason of bathhouse decree remains from mishnah power — do not immerse on bench and there impossible to say reason is fear for it deals with vessels — perforce wrote that mishnah forbade also from bathhouse decree — namely anything with vessel name even plain wood forbidden to immerse on since matter of receiving tumah; only on earthenware where no receiving tumah at all since impure only from airspace; and likewise silta which is wooden depression — we could permit; Rava came and said also on these do not immerse from fear; and practical difference between bathhouse decree and fear — all forbidden from bathhouse reason even after the fact for observer will err saying permitted in bathhouse; unlike fear reason all know she must immerse properly only concern lest fool fear and not immerse properly — for this they did not forbid after the fact — so appears practical difference; and therefore Raavad wrote as copied here in Shulchan Aruch — on anything receiving tumah do not immerse; and mentioned planks fit for treading for treading tumah applies only when intended for treading and sometimes needed practically as Rambam chapter 25 Laws of Vessels wrote — bathhouse planks smoothed do not receive tumah for not made for sitting, etc.; if so all the more plain planks sold for building certainly not impure from treading; but Raavad holds since fit for treading tumah if designated for that to make bench or similar — concern observer will err saying law applies all planks even if already designated for sitting and has treading tumah; and from this Raavad learned for anything fit to receive tumah; and Ritva and Beit Yosef bring him; and per this one should greatly wonder custom making planks in mikveh; Levush greatly wondered; and rabbis preceded namely responsum Maharam of Padua siman 31 when Maharar David Vitel asked why they do so and he answered at length — main point our planks in mikveh not considered designated for treading since made to stand to immerse — on contrary leave vessel category considered roof of house especially fixed with nails since initially no vessel on them; and further appears since planks placed on beams and water floats on them it is building — if so even if already vessels Scripturally even so now nullified, etc.; and his words very astounding for in Gemara we do not say bathhouse decree nullified except on silta wooden depression not wood even plain wood; and this is Raavad's main precision learning for anything fit to receive tumah; and further what merit to learn no vessel name initially — nevertheless observer will err; and how much written above therefore Raavad wrote fit for treading; and more astounding wrote even if vessel name initially nullified because water floats — is building — plain matter even in complete building building does not nullify vessel as in pipe they carved and afterward fixed disqualifies mikveh and he himself wrote so above in that responsum — how forgot in one statement; and further learned merit we hold those who hold plain wood does not receive treading tumah — this correct reason as will be explained; but not Raavad's view who wrote explicitly even plain wood and inferred from mentioning silta specifically; and in truth no complaint against Maharam in that responsum for he did not see Raavad and Rashba's words as he himself wrote there; and Mahari Chagiz wrote all Maharam of Padua's words are true and wonder on him who wrote main permission in being fixed with nails but if not fixed forbidden — but Maharam explicitly wrote to permit even unfixed that water makes them fixed; also Mahari Katz of Krakow dealt with this permission because vessel made for resting — meaning not for use only placed and does not receive tumah — likewise these planks; and per him permission even if not fixed with nails; and on him more to wonder for certainly receiving contact tumah not in resting vessel but treading tumah exists as I brought Rambam bathhouse planks — if designated for sitting certainly impure treading; summary — my mind not at rest with any permission for planks in mikveh per Raavad and Rashba above; and Levush properly wondered per chapter 7; however appears from another side we have clear permission — namely other poskim do not hold like Raavad here but hold in above Gemara Rava's bathhouse decree no prohibition from receiving tumah reason but other reasons not applicable to these planks; and proven from Rabbeinu Shem Tov Beit Yosef brings this siman — challenged mishnah spring passed over gutter — stone vessel fixed there disqualifies — why less than immersed vessel in vessel in mikveh if outer has mouth like spout of skin-bottle — resolved distinction between detached vessels valid to immerse when connected to mikveh versus fixed vessels spring flows over them lest come to error for sometimes fixed at water source without mouth like spout and all water passing over them invalid; and perhaps also reason of mishnah do not immerse on bench and also bathhouse decree above deals with pottery fixed to stand there because of mud and forbade from reason of separation — therefore his words in summary; and Rosh wrote on Rosh's words no difficulty for there inner vessel itself is immersed in mikveh since outer mouth like spout; but vessel connected to spring cannot immerse inside lest immerse in forty-seah vessel without connection — end quote; must understand Rosh's view in this distinction; and appears his view in vessel within vessel all immersed in mikveh no decree lest immerse in vessel without connection since immersing these two vessels in mikveh water is valid and no error drawn from this unlike spring flowing over gutter water always flows over it and sometimes connection of spring or mikveh breaks and fool will err immersing in it also then; and Beit Yosef wrote Rosh disagrees with Rosh that even detached do not immerse; nevertheless Rosh in responsum spout connected to mikveh floor — Rosh explained decree lest sometimes be at water source, etc.; and wrote in spout this decree does not apply and further plain spout has no holes and are plain wood not receiving tumah and do not disqualify by drawing as Beit Yosef brings nearby; and appears from his words clearly — one in spout decree lest make at water source does not apply for every spout has mouth like spout; and further we do not decree lest make at spring place except complete vessel whose purity depends on spout hole unlike plain wood not receiving tumah — therefore no concern will invalidate if made at water source; therefore no concern with these planks in our mikveh — clearly not worse than spout; and likewise Rosh's reason who forbade even detached lest immerse without connection — only gutter can immerse without connection to spring perhaps water remains unlike planks and spout if no connection to spring no spring — as appears clear and all the more per Beit Yosef's conclusion on Tur's words — impossible Rosh distinguished — Rosh's intent we do not care about receiving tumah only separate fear — plainly no concern with planks; and all I wrote regarding plank permission applies also to wooden beams in mikveh in all our countries one memory rises here and there; and I found Maharal of Prague wrote wooden rungs permitted since reason is decree lest lean bench to spring place and rungs not made for water crossing — therefore as I wrote; and therefore per custom certainly we do not hold in that Shulchan Aruch seif as ruled like Raavad who tied everything to receiving tumah and included plain wood and all fit for treading; rather like Rabbeinu Shem Tov and Rosh who per them complete permission in these planks and are exactly like spout connected to mikveh floor; and whoever disqualifies planks should also disqualify spout for also plain wood; and in truth both kosher without doubt even if planks placed without nail fixing — all permitted per Maharam of Padua and Mahari Katz; but not for their reasons rather as we said.`,
    '35#ב': `And need not distance, etc. Beit Yosef challenged — already gave other measure namely like plowshare, etc.; and appears Tur followed Gemara language R. Eliezer said she should immerse only derech gedilatah — meaning her walk as mishnah teaches man appears like digging and harvesting olives, etc.; and for negaim we say that measure for priest's viewing — likewise for immersion; but not every person expert in those measures how they are — gave measure every person can estimate herself how limbs somewhat distant as her walk; and both one measure; and likewise Tur's view after Gemara measure wrote since not every person can estimate thus will err saying must spread legs much and likewise distancing hands from body much to escape doubt — for this said need not only estimate as accustomed in walking; and Mahari Chagiz explained deals with modest women who raise hands on sides and distant way — that Talmud and poskim gave measure to such women daughters of Zion Scripture condemned.`,
  },
};
